  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOverview: Minimal changes required from the previous forecast \npackage with overall dry conditions prevailing today and tomorrow,\nbefore the arrival of a tropical airmass on Monday. \n\nWeekend...Ridging will maintain control over the region this \nweekend, with generally rain-free conditions prevailing. The \nexception being the eastern periphery of Hudspeth County which \ncould see isolated convection later today due to a weak passing \nmid-level disturbance combined with residual moisture (PWATs \n~1.2\").\n\nMonday-Wednesday Morning...Rain and thunderstorm chances increase\nsignificantly (60-90%) early next week in response to a moisture \ninflux over the area as the remnants of Polo merge with an upper- \nlevel closed low passing over the Southwest. Models are indicating\nrecord PWATs (1.4-1.6\") areawide Monday night into Tuesday which \nwill make it for another very active period. Heavy rainfall and \nflooding remain the main threats with this next passing system, as\nsoils are already saturated from this past week storms. Heavy \nrainfall could begin as early as Monday afternoon lasting through \nWednesday morning before clearing. Accumulations will most likely \nrange around 1-2\" across southern New Mexico and far West Texas \nwith localized amounts up to 3-4\". Although we currently are under\na Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall Monday and Tuesday, with the\nincreased flood concern and higher confidence in the forecast, \nanticipate these to be upgraded in later packages. A Flash Flood \nWatch will also need to be evaluated at a later package. Stay \ntuned for the latest forecast updates. \n\nWednesday afternoon into next weekend...Rain chances are forecast\nto come to an end through the day on Wednesday as the \naforementioned upper-level system drifts eastward and much drier \nair filters in behind it as ridging builds back up. The rest of \nnext week is shaping to be much quieter with plenty of sunshine \nand seasonable temperatures. \n\nFlood Safety...Flash Flooding can escalate quickly. Highly \nurbanized areas, arroyos, and low-water crossings will face the \nhighest risk of swift, dangerous water flows. Make sure to check \nthe forecast often over the coming days for the latest updates and\nhave at least two ways to received the Warnings. Never enter \nfloodwater, only 12\" of fast-moving water can carry away a car. \nTurn around, don't drown!",
